1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,275
And here is the idea we are proudest of.

2
00:00:03,225 --> 00:00:08,900
Most prototypes blur the line between a number that was computed and a number that was merely hoped for.

3
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:12,525
CoolSpend forces that line into the code itself.

4
00:00:12,525 --> 00:00:17,600
Every result comes from one of three backends, chosen by a single setting.

5
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:24,287
Mock is a clearly fake preview: it makes zero network calls and stamps every output "not measured data.

6
00:00:24,287 --> 00:00:34,037
Cached replays a real prior run, matched by a hash of the exact geometry; if there's no match, it raises an error — it never quietly falls back to the fake one.

7
00:00:34,037 --> 00:00:39,512
Live calls the real Infrared engine, then writes its answer to the cache for replay.

8
00:00:39,512 --> 00:00:45,450
And every number wears a provenance tag — verified, declared, or requires-verification.

9
00:00:45,450 --> 00:00:50,462
The interface will not say "measured" unless a real thermal grid sits behind it.

10
00:00:50,462 --> 00:00:54,862
The discipline is simple: never report a number we did not compute.

